      "question_type": "LONG",
      "stem_text": "Find the minimum value of \\[f(x) = \\sqrt{5 x^2 + 2 x \\sqrt{5} + 1} + x \\sqrt{5},\\]over all\n$x$ for which $f(x)$ is defined.",
      "options": null,
      "marks": 10,
      "gold_answer": "We can write \\begin{align*} f(x) &= \\sqrt{5 x^2 + 2 x \\sqrt{5} + 1} + x \\sqrt{5} \\\\ &=\n\\sqrt{(x \\sqrt{5} + 1)^2} + x \\sqrt{5} \\\\ &= |x \\sqrt{5} + 1| + x \\sqrt{5}. \\end{align*}If\n$x \\le -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},$ then \\[f(x) = |x \\sqrt{5} + 1| + x \\sqrt{5} = -x \\sqrt{5} - 1\n+ x \\sqrt{5} = -1.\\]If $x \\ge -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}},$ then \\begin{align*} f(x) &= |x\n\\sqrt{5} + 1| + x \\sqrt{5} \\\\ &= x \\sqrt{5} + 1 + x \\sqrt{5} \\\\ &= (x \\sqrt{5} + 1) + (x\n\\sqrt{5} + 1) - 1 \\\\ &\\ge -1. \\end{align*}Thus, the minimum value of $f(x)$ is\n$\\boxed{-1}.$",
